uans uh left in the last year  about 80 000 of those to the united  states that's unprecedented they're  going to costa rica so people are  fleeing the regime this there's a  widespread sense of fear and terror and  in that context this the the tools  available are not uh you know are not  are not great uh sanctions uh i don't  disagree with what's been said  that um it's unclear how effective  they're going to be i understand why  they're being they're being invoked and  applied but uh you know i'm certainly  not confident that's gonna that's gonna  make a change in ortega's uh  but i think there has to be more you  know i think international pressure is  important it's good  um but we know from experience that  international pressure  is  only  successful to the extent that there's an  internal  uh national  opposition force in nicaragua and today  that opposition force is largely in jail  the leaders are in jail  with no due process with no serious  charges with a just justice system  that's completely controlled by the  repressive regime and so first of all  there needs to be a regrouping uh of um  of some sort of uh opposition force  internally and um  the role that international that  external activists can play i think is  is limited but it can be helpful uh to  that end and so i think there needs to  be diplomatic i think more can be done  with the organization of american states  um there have been tough resolutions uh  but i think this could be more in  pressure internally and i'll just take  one other thing that i think is  important  that um you mentioned honduras honduras  will have a new government um  and that government i think is going to  be less supportive of ortega than the  previous government of guanoland or  juana linda hernandez and that may  affect  uh nicaragua's ability to get uh funds  and financing from the central american  bank if with with the change in  government honduras so there are  pressures i think from uh financial  institutio